"selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491971 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491971 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491981 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7f366c1-707a-566c-ae94-1cc85de0d171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42d1d2ed-a4fb-5a99-995b-31b25dd2611a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71c26421-0b8a-5150-b5ca-ed9ed8ecdd75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ Computing $A-(-6)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]+6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8da40f3d-5d18-5923-96f9-268309e6cbba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
